WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.501
From the Senate races that move

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.370
from governors races to Senate races.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:04.104
I mean,

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:05.805
thinking about the red wave

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:06.272
that never

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.942
manifested is causing a lot of reaction,

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.443
including Senator Ted Cruz,

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.911
who is pointing the finger

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:14.080
at the likes of Senator Mitch McConnell.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.749
Listen to what he says about being

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:19.285
while his words truly pissed off

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:21.121
Let me start off by saying

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:22.922
I am so pissed off,

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:24.591
I cannot even see straight.

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.526
The country is screwed

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:27.994
for the next four years

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:29.095
because of this

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:31.097
Mitch would rather be leader

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:35.101
than have a Republican majority.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.336
If there's a Republican

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.203
who can win, who's

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:39.372
not going to support Mitch

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:40.640
The truth of the matter is he'd

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.842
rather the Democrat win.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:45.145
Do you agree with him or not, Doug?

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:45.578
No.

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:46.646
And this is an argument

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:47.747
that you've had in certain parts

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.816
of conservatives circles for a few years.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:50.517
And Jim DeMint,

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:52.352
when he left the Senate

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:53.753
to go to the Heritage Foundation

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:54.387
and he was right

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:55.388
that he could be more powerful

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:56.956
outside the Senate than in it.

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.958
So I'd rather have 30 real conservatives

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:00.226
than a Republican majority.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.561
Well, Republican

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.229
majority is committee chairs,

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:04.364
and it's how you pass things

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:04.998
through the Senate

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:06.566
or through the House, for that matter,

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.968
of having those 30 conservative senators.

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:09.869
It's nice,

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:11.404
but it doesn't get you what you need to

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.439
to pass things through.

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:13.373
And ultimately,

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:15.075
McConnell was right here

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.409
when he said, we have a problem

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.178
with the quality of our candidates.

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:19.245
And that was true in the House

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.548
and the Senate and the governor's races.

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:22.482
And Cory Lake

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:24.150
should be an example for Republicans

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.652
now of what not to do.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:27.053
Don't tell your voters

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:28.521
to get the hell out of the party

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.056
because they're going to do that.

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:31.624
You know, one thing about the idea of

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.759
who might have been right

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:33.159
or maybe

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.627
vindicated in a subtle way

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.330
here is Congresswoman Liz Cheney, right?

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.400
Who has made a great deal of discussions

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.234
and points

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:42.469
about the idea of trying

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:44.370
to reclaim the Republican Party.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.271
Listen to what she had to say

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.239
when she was speaking.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:47.640
University of Chicago

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.641
just a few days ago

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.877
about what Republicans she said

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:51.511
need to do

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.615
Do you feel like there's

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:58.418
a stiff enough spine in the party

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:01.888
to stand up against future insurrections?

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:04.224
Or are we worried about January 6th?

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:06.159
20. 25?

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.161
No, we definitely need stiffer spines.

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:10.096
I mean, that's for sure.

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:12.866
And I think that

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.634
I think elected officials

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.936
need to understand that words matter.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:20.807
And when you you sort of

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.342
see again and again

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:22.876
and again

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:24.144
people accepting things

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:25.612
that are indefensible.

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:27.947
You know,
we watch what happens in our society.

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.150
And when you accept things

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:31.284
that you shouldn't

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.752
when you tolerate things

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.154
like January six,

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:36.422
then that can very easily

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:37.557
become the new normal.

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:39.592
Then it begins to be legal.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.228
And I think that's a big

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.030
a big danger for us.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:44.831
I mean, it's kind of fine

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.232
without actually to see her

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.667
sort of lurking in the shadows there,

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.202
because in many ways,

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.271
her shadow was cast on these elections

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:53.106
as somebody who even supported Democrats

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:54.240
in some instances

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.309
because she wanted people to understand

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:58.111
about democracy being on the line.

00:02:58.111 --> 00:02:59.012
But her point is,

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.980
Republicans need to have stiffer spines

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.849
in order to even compete

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:03.483
in a future election.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:05.084
You talk with a long game a lot.

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.386
Do they need swift response

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:07.320
and do they actually have it?

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:09.322
I don't know if

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:10.657
Republicans have it or not.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:12.292
I do think

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:14.827
they need to condemn Donald Trump

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:16.863
across the board.

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:17.997
I think the reason why

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.265
you're seeing this split is

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.401
because there are there's still you know.

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:22.802
30% of Republicans

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:25.071
that support Donald Trump.

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:26.372
The election deniers

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.841
did not win this election, but they did.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:30.276
Some of them didn't get blown out.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.344
You mentioned

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:32.312
we're a week out

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:33.146
and we were waiting

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:35.348
for every single ballot to be counted

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:36.449
because there was still

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:37.350
a significant amount

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.918
of people in Arizona

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:39.352
who thought

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:41.321
Carrie Lake should be the governor.

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:42.822
And I think that's what Liz Cheney is

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:43.756
speaking to.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:44.290
I agree.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.125
I don't think anyone when the court

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:49.329
says, no, no, no, you did not win

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:50.730
and you don't have legal recourse,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.932
but we can make it commonplace

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:56.069
where election deniers can still thrive

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:57.770
and be actual candidates,

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.540
because when they are let's rewind.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.507
A year or so ago,

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.009
we have people like the cyber

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:05.144
ninjas wasting taxpayer dollars

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.813
running these sham

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:08.448
fraudulent audits that we know

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:10.116
didn't actually need to happen.

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:11.951
And the results still stayed the same.

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:14.420
So I don't know if Republicans

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:14.954
will have a stiff

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.090
enough spine to say no to Donald Trump.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:17.824
We'll see tomorrow

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:18.891
after this announcement.

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.260
I mean, if he announces. Right.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:20.860
And part of the issue,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.495
I mean,
the idea of having a spine, Alex,

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:26.032
these issues here and instances is it

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:28.468
really is contingent on whether

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:29.235
the so-called leader

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.503
of the Republican Party,

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:31.738
Donald Trump,

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:33.706
will vie to be the next president

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.441
yet again. And just listen to this.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:36.342
You had a number of people

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.076
who have been saying

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:37.944
just today alone

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:39.646
that Trump is bad for the party

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:41.648
from Governor Baker to Larry Hogan.

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:42.348
Listen to this.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:45.385
I think one of the

00:04:45.385 --> 00:04:47.387
messages from the election

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:50.723
is for Republicans generally is

00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:54.827
we need we need as a party

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.930
to move past

00:04:58.398 --> 00:04:59.265
President Trump,

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:01.334
especially the third election in a row

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:04.237
that Donald Trump has cost us the race.

00:05:04.237 --> 00:05:05.104
And it's like, you know,

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:06.072
three strikes, you're out.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.041
I think what Republicans came to grips

00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:08.574
with Tuesday

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:10.576
night was we're tired of losing

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.111
and we're tired of Donald Trump.

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:13.313
Dragging us to lose

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:14.781
because of his personal vanity.

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.849
President Trump had to insert himself.

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:18.551
There's a very high correlation

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:22.088
between MAGA candidates and big losses

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:24.824
or at least dramatically underperforming.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:27.827
I mean, but having said that,

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:30.063
will the Republican Party quit Trump?

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:31.698
Well, what all four of those men

00:05:31.698 --> 00:05:32.465
have in common

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:33.900
is that they're not facing

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.602
Republican primary voters again

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:38.071
any time soon.

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:38.938
Maybe Larry Hogan

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.074
will run for president in 20, 24

00:05:41.074 --> 00:05:42.975
and we'll see how kind of how that

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:44.344
is for them then.

00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:45.411
But yeah, I mean,

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:46.546
you know,
Charlie Baker

00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:47.980
declined to run for reelection

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:48.815
rather than face

00:05:48.815 --> 00:05:50.917
a Trump endorsed primary challenger.

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.151
And that cost

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:55.021
the GOP really badly in Massachusetts.

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:57.090
I think the voices that it's worth

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.891
really watching out for

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:02.595
are the Republicans who got reelected

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:04.597
this month

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:07.100
without playing the Trump game.

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:07.667
Right.

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.802
So someone like Brian Kemp in Georgia,

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:11.938
Trump made it his personal mission

00:06:11.938 --> 00:06:13.539
to destroy him

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:14.874
because he didn't try to overturn

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.342
the 2020 election.

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:16.809
And first,

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:19.045
Brian Kemp demolished David Perdue

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:20.880
in the Republican primary for governor

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.081
and then he beat Stacey Abrams

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:23.950
by a very solid margin

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:24.951
in the general election.

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.120
Does somebody like him speak up and say,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.621
I know how to win,

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:30.390
I've done it better than Donald Trump

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.091
and it's time for the party to move on?

00:06:32.091 --> 00:06:33.593
He hasn't said that kind of thing yet.

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:35.094
I do think that's the kind of voice

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.596
of Mike DeWine in Ohio,

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:37.730
Greg Abbott in Texas.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:38.965
If you start hearing from people

00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:39.632
who are

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:40.767
frankly closer

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:43.669
to the center of the Republican Party

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:45.104
rather than the center of the country,

00:06:45.104 --> 00:06:47.440
which is where Charlie Baker is,

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:48.574
then I think that would send

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:49.742
a pretty powerful message

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:50.810
to Republican voters.

